package promoted_literal_call_arg_refused;

fn id(p: &i64) -> &i64 {
    return p;
}

fn main() -> i32 {
    let a = id(&5i64);
    if *a != 5i64 { return 1i32; }
    return 0i32;
}
// SOUNDNESS QUEUE row promoted_literal_call_arg_refused (tier 3).
// Legal Rust: `&5i64` is a constant expression and is promoted to a `&'static i64`
// (rvalue static promotion), so the reference `id` returns outlives the statement.
// Legality MEASURED with rustc 1.98.1 (foot of this file); the promotion rule as
// read agrees, and `let a: &i64 = &5i64;` (the same literal WITHOUT the call) compiles and
// runs 5 today. TODAY refused: "temporary value dropped while borrowed: this reference
// borrows into a temporary that is dropped at the end of the statement; bind the
// owning value to a variable first so it outlives the borrow" — also when `a` is
// never used, and also when the parameter is `&'static i64`. Measured 2026-09-13c
// on builds 86911f4ef2b44caf (unarmed hand m2l_promoted_and_field / split_promoted) and
// fef29e3403b8e8fd (p1, p3, p6). `const_promote::is_promoted_borrow` answers `&5i64` and
// `&id(3i64)` (pass fixture bc_tmpassign_promoted) but a promoted literal passed AS A
// CALL ARGUMENT whose result is bound is not recognised.
// rustc 1.98.1 --edition 2024 (round 2026-09-15f-rustcaudit, class CONFIRMED): COMPILES and RUNS exit 0. Twin src/compiler/probes/2026-09-15f-rustcaudit/rust/promoted_literal_call_arg_refused.rs. Audit note: `&5i64` promoted as a call arg.
